<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lenape</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Delaware people, Lenni Lenape</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lenape, also called the Delaware, are an Algonquian-speaking Indigenous people whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a> covered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delaware-river">Delaware River</a> valley, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a> and the lower Hudson valley, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan">Manhattan</a>. They traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and Swedish colonists in the 17th century and made treaties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-penn">William Penn</a>, but lost most of their land, notably through the Walking Purchase of 1737. Pushed westward, most of their descendants now live in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisconsin">Wisconsin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lenape.jpg" alt="Lenape" width="320" height="484" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User:Nikater, 1 Feb 2007 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lenape01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
